      text: `Ask people where the oxygen they breathe comes from, and most will point to forests. Yet roughly half of the oxygen produced on Earth each year comes from organisms most of us have never seen. Phytoplankton are microscopic plant-like organisms that drift near the surface of oceans, lakes and rivers. Like land plants, they use sunlight, water and carbon dioxide to make their food, releasing oxygen in the process. Their importance does not end there. Tiny animals feed on phytoplankton, small fish eat those animals, and larger fish, seabirds and whales depend on the small fish. Almost all life in the sea therefore rests on this invisible base. Phytoplankton also absorb large amounts of carbon dioxide; when they die, some sink to the ocean floor and take that carbon with them, helping to slow the warming of the planet. However, they are sensitive to change. Warmer surface water mixes less with the cooler, nutrient-rich water below, which can reduce their growth. At the same time, fertiliser washed off farms can cause sudden explosions of certain species, known as blooms, which use up the oxygen in the water as they decay and kill fish.`,
