      text: `For thousands of years, millets such as jowar, bajra and ragi fed families across the drier parts of India. These hardy grains grow on poor soils, need far less water than rice and can survive high temperatures. They are also rich in fibre, iron and calcium. Yet after the Green Revolution of the 1960s, government support, high-yielding seeds and cheap supply through ration shops were directed mainly towards rice and wheat. Millets came to be seen as coarse food for the poor, and the area under their cultivation shrank sharply. Now the tide is turning. Concern about diabetes and poor nutrition has led doctors to recommend whole grains, and falling water tables have made farmers look again at crops that need little irrigation. At India's suggestion, the United Nations declared 2023 the International Year of Millets. Several states have added millets to school meals and public distribution, and city shops sell millet biscuits, noodles and flour. Still, challenges remain. Millets are harder to process than polished rice, farmers need assured buyers before they switch crops, and many young people simply do not know how to cook them. A revival will need markets and kitchens, not just slogans.`,
