      text: `In the town of Bishnupur in Bankura district, rows of temples glow a warm reddish brown in the evening sun. They were built mostly in the seventeenth and eighteenth centuries by the Malla kings, who ruled this part of western Bengal for several hundred years. Unlike the great stone temples of Odisha or south India, these are made largely of baked bricks and laterite, because good building stone was scarce in the region. The builders turned this limitation into an art. Their walls are covered in terracotta panels, each a small tile of fired clay showing scenes from the Ramayana and the Mahabharata, the life of Krishna, hunting parties, boats and soldiers. Many temples copy the curved roofs of Bengali village huts, a shape that sheds heavy monsoon rain. The Jor Bangla temple, for instance, looks like two such huts joined together, while the Rasmancha has a pyramid-shaped roof surrounded by arched passages. Bishnupur is also known for the Baluchari sari, woven with stories from the epics, and for its own school of classical music. Together these traditions show how a small kingdom created a culture that still draws visitors centuries later.`,
