      text: `Chandrasekhara Venkata Raman was a brilliant student who completed his master's degree in physics at a very young age. Since India offered few research jobs at the time, he joined the government's finance department in Calcutta as an officer. Yet his love of science did not fade. After office hours, he carried out experiments at the Indian Association for the Cultivation of Science, a small institution with modest equipment. Eventually he left his secure post to become a professor of physics at Calcutta University. On a voyage to Europe in 1921, Raman began wondering why the sea looked so deeply blue. A common explanation held that it merely reflected the sky, but he suspected that water itself scattered light. Back in Calcutta, he and his student K. S. Krishnan studied how light changes when it passes through liquids. On 28 February 1928 they announced that a small part of the scattered light changes its colour, or wavelength, depending on the molecules it meets. This discovery, known as the Raman effect, gave scientists a new way to study the structure of substances. In 1930 Raman received the Nobel Prize in Physics, the first Asian to win a Nobel Prize in science. India now celebrates 28 February as National Science Day.`,
