      text: `On 29 July 1911, a crowd of tens of thousands gathered around a football ground in Calcutta. Many could not see the pitch at all and depended on news passed back from those in front, and some kites were flown to signal the score. The final of the IFA Shield was being played between Mohun Bagan, an Indian club, and the East Yorkshire Regiment, a British army team. No Indian club had ever won the tournament. Most of the Mohun Bagan players were barefoot, while their opponents wore heavy boots. The British side scored first, but Mohun Bagan equalised and then scored the winner to win by two goals to one. The city erupted in celebration. The victory came at a time when Bengal had been stirred by the protests against its partition, and many Indians saw it as proof that they could defeat their rulers on equal terms. Newspapers hailed the team as heroes, and the win gave a powerful boost to football across Bengal. Today 29 July is remembered by the club as Mohun Bagan Day, and the match is often described as a moment when sport and national pride became deeply connected.`,
